module gunbc.sibling_operand_effect_site_census

import std.types { Bool, Int, List, NonEmptyStr }
import v2.std.algebra { filter, length }


// THE POPULATION AN UNLANDED WALL WOULD REFUSE, COUNTED BEFORE THE WALL EXISTS.
//
// The class is gunbc.recurring_failure_mode
// sibling_operand_effect_order_is_underdetermined_and_unenforced: an accepted program in which two
// siblings of ONE operand list each reach an observable effect, so its meaning depends on which
// runs first, while every authority the realizations consult declines to carry that order. The
// derivation read on that row concluded that the remedy is a REFUSAL rather than a declared order,
// and the owning lane admitted that refusal only behind this census (snappy-deer-443, 2026-09-22):
// a refusal enrolled without the count cannot distinguish a wall that fires on nothing from a wall
// that breaks the corpus, and the count is the only thing that says which.
//
// THE PREDICATE CENSUSED, stated once so every row is read against the same proposition: a sibling
// operand list -- an argument list or a record literal's field list -- more than one of whose
// DIRECT children is a call to a function DECLARING a `uses` resource requirement. Declared, not
// reached: a sibling that reaches an effect through a call chain it does not itself declare is
// OUTSIDE this predicate and outside the narrower wall, which is why that wall is described on the
// class row as narrower rather than weaker, and why the trigger retiring the row stays named at the
// call-reachability capability v2.std.effect_reach already carries.

// WHICH SIBLING LIST THE SITE IS, and it is a distinction with a consequence rather than a label.
// Under v2.std.node connective_edge_discipline a record literal's fields are LabeledEdges, and
// canonicalize_labeled_for_content_hash sorts labeled children by label before hashing -- so two
// spellings of a RecordLiteralFields site differing only in field order are THE SAME NODE under
// content_hash while their effect order differs. Every row in this census is a named-sibling list,
// so that applies to all of them; the kind is carried because an argument list is the form the
// authored ruling on gunbc.host_effect_realize srv3_chown_then_read_back was written about, and a
// reader checking this census against that ruling needs to see which rows it literally covers.
type SiblingListKind
  = ArgumentList
  | RecordLiteralFields
  | InfixConnectiveOperands

// WHETHER REORDERING THE SIBLINGS WOULD CHANGE WHAT THE PROGRAM MEANS, which is the question the
// owning lane asked for and is NOT the same question as whether the wall refuses the site. The wall
// refuses every row here regardless of this field, because the order is undeclared in all of them.
// This field says what is at stake if a realization reorders them, and the three arms are ordered
// worst first.
//
// OrderObservablyMatters is the arm that makes the class urgent: the siblings touch ONE subject, so
// a reordering changes the answer the program computes and no row says which answer is right.
//
// OrderNotObservableAtTodaysArguments is the arm a reader will want to treat as a pass and must
// not. The siblings touch disjoint subjects TODAY, so no reordering visible today changes the
// result -- but that is a property of the arguments this call happens to be given, not a declared
// fact about the node, and nothing refuses a future caller who makes them overlap. It is the
// agreement-without-an-authority shape the class row names as the most dangerous, at site grain.
//
// PredicateFalsePositive is the honest escape and is load-bearing: a row here means the text sweep
// found a shape the MODELED predicate would not, and leaving it in the roster rather than deleting
// it is what lets a reader check the sweep instead of trusting it.
type SiblingOrderStake
  = OrderObservablyMatters { shared_subject: NonEmptyStr }
  | OrderNotObservableAtTodaysArguments { why_disjoint_today: NonEmptyStr }
  | PredicateFalsePositive { why_the_model_excludes_it: NonEmptyStr }

// ONE DISCOVERED SITE.
//
// `effect_reaching_children` and `callees` are two views of one fact and the witness beside this
// carrier pins them to each other, because a row whose count disagrees with its list is a row a
// reader cannot check against the source.
//
// `callee_resolution_is_unambiguous` is not decoration and it is the one property of this sweep
// that was MEASURED rather than assumed: every callee name appearing in any row below has EXACTLY
// ONE function declaration corpus-wide, and that declaration is the uses-declaring one. A text
// sweep cannot resolve a name, so a homonym would have made the whole census unsound; the
// uniqueness check is what stands in for resolution here, and a future row whose name is not unique
// must carry false and be dispositioned rather than counted.
type SiblingEffectSite {
  site_module: NonEmptyStr
  enclosing_declaration: NonEmptyStr
  list_kind: SiblingListKind
  parent_construct: NonEmptyStr
  effect_reaching_children: Int
  callees: List<NonEmptyStr>
  callee_resolution_is_unambiguous: Bool
  stake: SiblingOrderStake
}

// THE ROSTER. Fourteen sites, all in the product layer under dag/gunbc, all NAMED-sibling lists.
